= Introduction

== Overview

*Calisthenics* is an exacting and niche sport. Unlike regular hypertrophy training, Calisthenics requires "good form", which mandates a high level of kinesthetic awareness; and unlike other sports, Calisthenics is demanding on the central nervous system (CNS), which disallows frequent voluminous sessions. Calisthenics has not received adequate scientific attention. Most beginners rely on experienced athletes for advice, which, due to anatomical disparity, might not be appropriate for them. Although it is rising in popularity, the sport is still not an "official" one, so "official" guidelines are still non-existent. People start Calisthenics due to its affordability and accessibility, but advanced movements necessitate time-consuming personal exploration or expensive coaching. 

Given these complications, a reliable spotter, a systematic program, and a detailed log are non-negotiable. All of these are frustrating burdens that sometimes deprive the training of its carefree enjoyment. 

*Isometry* aims to reduce the friction in the fragmented administrative process by collapsing these drudgeries into a concentrated platform. Isometry's interface emphasizes organizational clarity

